    {
      "id": "language.amount-in-words",
      "title": {
        "fr": "Montants en lettres",
        "ar": "كتابة المبالغ بالحروف",
        "en": "Amounts in words"
      },
      "description": "Words used to write DZD amounts on invoices and cheques. French uses the traditional spelling: \"vingt\" and \"cent\" take an s only when multiplied and at the end of the number (quatre-vingts, deux cents, but quatre-vingt-un, deux cent mille); \"mille\" never takes an s; \"million\" and \"milliard\" are nouns (deux millions) and take \"de\" before the currency when nothing follows (un million de dinars). Arabic chooses the form of the counted noun from the last two digits of the number: 1 singular followed by واحد, 2 dual, 3–10 plural (the number takes the ة form), 11–99 singular accusative, otherwise singular. The vectors in vectors/words-*.json are the reference.\n",
      "versions": [
        {
